syntax: a ParseError names the text that closes the construct left open

A program completing partial input, such as a shell prompt or an editor
completing the word under the cursor, wants to parse the input up to the
cursor and read the syntax tree there. RecoverErrors closes some
constructs left open at the end of the input, but not all of them: a
test expression, a subscript, a slice, zsh parameter flags, modifiers
and glob qualifiers still fail with "reached EOF without matching", and
the caller cannot tell from the message text what would close them.

ParseError gains Closer, the text which was missing to close the
construct starting at Pos: its closing token, on a new line when that is
a reserved word such as "fi" or "]]". When the input ended inside the
construct, Closer starts with what error recovery supplied at the end of
the input for the constructs inside it, so appending Closer and parsing
again moves on to the next construct out, until the input parses:

	_, err := NewParser(RecoverErrors(8)).Parse(strings.NewReader(`[[ "$a`), "")
	fmt.Printf("%q\n", err.(ParseError).Closer) // "\"\n]]"

